Appendicitis
Young Adult & Adolecence
Migratory RLQ pain, Anorexia, McBurney's point tenderness

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-004
Reason for Visit: Acute, progressively worsening right lower quadrant abdominal pain accompanied by persistent nausea and anorexia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an acute onset of migrating abdominal pain, vomiting, and inability to ambulate due to severe localized pain. Urgent after-hours assessment was critically necessary to perform a physical examination to rule out an acute surgical abdomen, specifically appendicitis, and to facilitate immediate escalation to emergency surgical services to prevent appendiceal rupture and peritonitis prior to the next regular GP session.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The pain started around my belly button yesterday, but now it's shifted to my lower right side and hurts so much if I even cough."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 24-hour history of abdominal discomfort that began as vague, migratory periumbilical pain. Over the last 6 hours, the pain has firmly localized to the right lower quadrant, increasing sharply in intensity. The patient reports profound anorexia and loss of appetite since the pain began, followed by three episodes of non-bilious nausea and vomiting. The pain is severely exacerbated by movement, walking, or coughing. The patient reports feeling feverish and unwell.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound; unable to stand upright due to acute abdominal pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of rapid clinical deterioration and perforation if definitive surgical management is delayed.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/68 | HR: 105 | Temp: 38.1 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, diaphoretic, and in acute distress, lying entirely still with knees drawn up to the chest to minimize peritoneal irritation.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals localized, severe tenderness in the right lower quadrant. There is exquisite rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Involuntary localized guarding and rigidity are present. A positive Rovsing's sign is elicited (pain in the right lower quadrant upon palpation of the left lower quadrant). Psoas and obturator signs are positive. Bowel sounds are diminished. No palpable masses. Respiratory and cardiovascular exams are unremarkable except for tachycardia.
Environmental Observations: A bucket is kept beside the bed due to ongoing emesis.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient took Paracetamol 1000mg 4 hours ago with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Appendicitis - Status: Exacerbated/Surgical Emergency
Differentials:

Mesenteric Adenitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the highly localized peritoneal signs (guarding, rigidity, rebound tenderness at McBurney's point), which strongly favor appendiceal involvement over generalized adenopathy.

Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the classical migratory nature of the pain, the profound localization to the right lower quadrant, and the absence of profuse watery diarrhea.

Renal Colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of fever, anorexia, and true peritonism, rather than the colicky, writhing flank-to-groin pain typical of a ureteric calculus.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical Risk: Extremely High)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic triad of migratory right lower quadrant pain, profound anorexia, and McBurney's point tenderness, combined with objective signs of localized peritonitis (fever, guarding, rebound tenderness, positive Rovsing's sign). This clinical picture is highly indicative of an acutely inflamed, and potentially impending ruptured, appendix. Delaying this assessment until standard in-hours care was categorically unsafe; the immediate after-hours home visit confirmed an acute surgical abdomen that mandates immediate emergency department transfer to prevent catastrophic localized perforation, generalized peritonitis, and septic shock.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate escalation and transfer to the Emergency Department for surgical evaluation, nil by mouth (NBM) status enforced. Anticipated need for transabdominal ultrasound or CT scanning to identify an enlarged non-compressible appendix greater than 6 mm with periappendiceal fat stranding. Intravenous fluids, analgesia, and antiemetics to be provided upon ED arrival.
Positive Behavioral Change: To facilitate surgical preparation, strictly maintain a nil by mouth status (no food or water), explaining that an empty stomach ensures safe administration of general anesthesia during surgery. To minimize pain during transport, brace the abdomen with a pillow when coughing or moving, directly reducing movement-induced peritoneal friction.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None. Immediate ED transfer.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Post-operative routine GP follow-up 7 days post-discharge for wound check and recovery assessment.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via private vehicle (driven by parents) is initiated; if the patient's condition acutely declines (loss of consciousness, intractable vomiting) during transport preparation, call 000 for an ambulance.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a surgical emergency pending definitive imaging and surgical review at the hospital.
Clinical Course: The condition will inevitably worsen without surgical intervention; prompt removal of the appendix will lead to full recovery, whereas delay risks life-threatening rupture.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Proceed directly to the nearest Emergency Department immediately. Do not eat, drink, or take any further oral medications. Call 000 if the pain suddenly becomes widespread across the entire stomach or if you feel faint.

